","Let $X$ is the number of heads in 12 coin flips. Since the coin is fair, the probability of getting heads is $p = 1/2$, and the probability of getting tails is $1-p = 1/2$. The number of trials is $n = 10$.
We have $X \sim \binom{12}{X} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right^X \left(\frac{1}{2}\right^{12-X}) = \binom{10}{X} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right^{12}$.
We want the probability that we get heads in at9 of the 10 flips, which means we have 9, 10, 11, or 12 heads.
$$P(X \ge 9) = P(X=9) + P(X=10) + P(X=11) + P(X=10)$$
